const DEEP_QA = [
  { k: 'FluxCore', query: 'fluxcore what is it provider',
    q: ['what is fluxcore', 'whats the difference between fluxcore and a fluxnode', 'how do i rent out my gpu'],
    url: 'https://docs.runonflux.com/fluxcore/overview',
    doc: 'FluxCore is a desktop application that contributes a machine idle GPU and CPU to the FluxEdge marketplace, combining native Flux mining with pay-as-you-go hardware rental. FluxNodes are providers for FluxCloud; FluxCore is the provider side of FluxEdge. Supported operating systems are Windows 10 and 11 and Ubuntu 22.04, not Ubuntu Minimal.',
    a: 'FluxCore is the **provider** application for FluxEdge: you install it on a machine and it offers that machine\'s GPU and CPU for rent, and mines with it when nobody has rented it [1].\n\nThe distinction from a FluxNode is the product each one supplies. A FluxNode supplies FluxCloud, hosts other people\'s containers and requires locked collateral. FluxCore supplies FluxEdge, rents your GPU by the hour and requires **no collateral at all**, just the hardware.\n\nWindows 10 or 11, or Ubuntu 22.04. Not Ubuntu Minimal, which is missing dependencies it needs.',
    fu: [
      { u: 'how do i install it on linux', doc: 'Linux install runs bash -i with the setup script from download.fluxcore.ai. Enter 1 to install prerequisites, give the sudo password, and select the local IP. The UI is then at http://<local-ip>:18180. NVIDIA 570 series drivers and both CUDA packages must be installed before FluxCore, or GPU benchmarks fall back to CPU and a full reinstall is needed.',
        a: 'One command, but there is an order that matters [1]:\n\n```sh\nbash -i <(curl -s https://download.fluxcore.ai/setup.sh)\n```\n\nPick 1 to install prerequisites, give your sudo password, choose the machine\'s local IP, and the interface comes up at `http://<your-ip>:18180`.\n\nThe trap: **install the NVIDIA driver and both CUDA packages first**. If CUDA goes on after FluxCore, GPU benchmarking silently falls back to CPU and the only fix is a full uninstall and reinstall. Uninstalling is option 2 in the same script.' },
      { u: 'can i set my own price', doc: 'Providers cannot set their own price. The hourly price is computed from teraflops, VRAM speed, RAM speed, RAM size, storage, disk write speed, network speeds, lease completion reliability and operating system, normalised against network minimum and maximum and clamped into a band. Provider payout is price per hour times duration, minus penalties, minus the InFlux commission.',
        a: 'No, and this surprises people coming from other marketplaces [1]. The hourly rate is computed from your machine: teraflops, VRAM and RAM speed, storage, disk write speed, network, operating system, and your lease-completion reliability, normalised against the rest of the network.\n\nYour payout is that rate times the duration, minus penalties, minus the InFlux commission. The commission percentage is not published in the docs, so I will not guess at it.\n\nThe lever you do control is reliability. It feeds the price directly.' },
      { u: 'what happens if my machine reboots during a rental', doc: 'A reboot, internet outage or similar during a lease stops the rental, damages the reliability score and forfeits 20 percent of accrued rewards for that lease, refunded to the customer. The machine has 5 minutes to come back online or it is removed from the cluster and must be manually rejoined.',
        a: 'It costs you three ways [1]. The rental stops. Your reliability score drops, which lowers your future price. And you forfeit **20% of the rewards accrued on that lease**, refunded to the customer.\n\nThen there is a five-minute clock: back online inside it and you stay in the cluster, miss it and the machine is removed and you have to rejoin manually.\n\nWhich is the argument for not running FluxCore on the machine you also game on and reboot for Windows updates.' },
    ] },
  { k: 'FluxEdge renting', query: 'fluxedge rent gpu machine projects',
    q: ['how do i rent a gpu on fluxedge', 'what is a project on fluxedge', 'can i train a model on fluxedge'],
    url: 'https://docs.runonflux.com/fluxedge/projects/rent-machine',
    doc: 'The Rent Machine interface has three tabs: Premium, Dedicated GPU and Dedicated CPU. A project is a deployment, a Kubernetes pod, placed on a rented machine, created by Quick Launch from pre-configured apps or by Custom Deployment. Custom Deployment takes a name, Docker image, ports, domains, environment variables, commands and resources, mirrored into an editable Kubernetes YAML. Unlimited projects can run on one leased machine at no extra cost. Machines can be rented for any duration including seconds, hours or days.',
    a: 'You pick a machine, then you put projects on it [1].\n\nThree kinds of machine: **Premium**, which is third-party enterprise hardware and needs KYC level 1 unlocked by a fiat deposit; **Dedicated GPU**; and **Dedicated CPU** for work that does not need an accelerator. You can filter on VRAM, cores, network speed and GPU model, and sort by score per dollar per hour.\n\nA **project** is a Kubernetes pod on the machine you leased. Quick Launch gives you pre-configured apps; Custom Deployment gives you image, ports, environment, commands and resources, with the generated YAML editable if you want it.\n\nYou pay for the machine, not per project, so you can run as many projects on one lease as it will hold. Rental granularity is arbitrary, down to seconds.\n\nTraining and inference are both in scope. That is what the GPUs are there for.',
    fu: [
      { u: 'is my data safe on there if the lease ends', doc: 'Local Storage is a volume on the provider disk, persistent only for the duration of the lease. It is lost on migration and is not persisted across stop and relaunch even on the same provider. There are no shared volumes and one volume per container.',
        a: 'Treat the lease as the lifetime of the data [1]. Local Storage lives on the provider\'s disk and persists only while the lease runs. It does not survive a migration, and it does not survive stop and relaunch even on the same machine.\n\nSo: checkpoint to somewhere off the machine during a long training run, and pull your outputs before you stop the lease. There are no shared volumes, and one volume per container.' },
      { u: 'can i change the specs after i deploy', doc: 'You cannot upgrade a project specs after deployment. Container resource limits are not enforced, and the application container uses as many resources as it needs on the dedicated machine. Deployment logs are deleted after 10 days.',
        a: 'No, a project\'s specs are fixed once deployed [1]. You would redeploy it.\n\nTwo related things from the same page. Container resource limits are not actually enforced, so a project on a dedicated machine can use whatever the machine has, which is generous rather than dangerous when the machine is yours for the lease. And deployment logs are deleted after 10 days, so pull anything you need to keep.' },
      { u: 'how does billing work', doc: 'Billing is pay as you go, deducted from a prepaid FluxEdge balance continuously and in real time. Deposits are non-refundable and non-withdrawable; the balance system acts as a top-up account and any deposited amount must be spent. Deposit methods are PayPal, Stripe card, FLUX and voucher codes. A low balance warning triggers when the balance cannot cover 24 hours at the current spend rate.',
        a: 'Prepaid, drawn down continuously in real time while a lease runs [1].\n\nThe thing to know before you top up: **deposits are non-refundable and non-withdrawable**. The balance is a top-up account and whatever goes in has to be spent there. Deposit what you plan to use rather than parking money in it.\n\nPayPal, card via Stripe, FLUX or a voucher code. There is a low-balance warning that fires when your balance will not cover another 24 hours at the current rate, which is worth enabling before a long run.' },
    ] },
  { k: 'private registry', query: 'repoauth private registry aws ecr azure google',
    q: ['how do i deploy an image from our private registry', 'can i use aws ecr with flux', 'how do i authenticate to a private docker registry'],
    url: 'https://github.com/runonflux/flux/blob/master/docs/registry-auth/REPOAUTH_STRING_FORMAT.md',
    doc: 'Registry credentials go in the component repoauth field, which must be a string and never an object. Basic auth is username:password. Cloud providers use provider://param=value form: aws-ecr://region=REGION&accessKeyId=KEY&secretAccessKey=SECRET, azure-acr://clientId=ID&clientSecret=SECRET&tenantId=TENANT, google-gar://keyFile=BASE64_ENCODED_JSON. Tokens are cached in memory only and never written to disk. For v8 apps the credentials live in an encrypted enterprise blob. Token validity is 12 hours for AWS ECR, 3 hours for Azure ACR and 60 minutes for Google GAR.',
    a: 'The component\'s **repoauth** field, and it is per-component, so one app can mix a public image with a private one [1].\n\nIt is always a string, never an object. Basic auth for a plain registry is `username:password`. Cloud registries use a scheme:\n\n```\naws-ecr://region=eu-west-2&accessKeyId=AKIA...&secretAccessKey=...\nazure-acr://clientId=...&clientSecret=...&tenantId=...\ngoogle-gar://keyFile=<base64 of the service account JSON>\n```\n\nSupplying repoauth makes the app an enterprise app, so the compose section is encrypted and only ArcaneOS nodes can decrypt it. The cloud tokens FluxOS fetches are held in memory only and never written to disk, and they are short-lived: 12 hours for ECR, 3 for ACR, 60 minutes for GAR.\n\nWhat you must not do is put the credentials in an environment variable. Environment parameters are part of the public specification.',
    fu: [
      { u: 'what iam permissions does it need', doc: 'The minimal AWS IAM policy is ecr:GetAuthorizationToken, ecr:BatchGetImage and ecr:GetDownloadUrlForLayer. Azure requires the AcrPull role. Google requires roles/artifactregistry.reader scoped to specific repositories.',
        a: 'Read-only pull rights, nothing more [1]:\n\n- **AWS**: `ecr:GetAuthorizationToken`, `ecr:BatchGetImage`, `ecr:GetDownloadUrlForLayer`\n- **Azure**: the `AcrPull` role\n- **Google**: `roles/artifactregistry.reader`, scoped to the specific repositories rather than the project\n\nCreate a dedicated principal for this rather than reusing a developer\'s credentials, and scope it to the one repository the app pulls.' },
      { u: 'is it safe to commit the spec to git', a: 'An encrypted spec is, yes, and the docs say so explicitly [1]. The credentials in a v8 app live inside the encrypted enterprise blob, so the registered specification does not carry them in the clear.\n\nBasic auth is the weaker case even so: the credentials are long-lived and sent on every Docker operation with no rotation. If you have the option, use the cloud provider schemes, because those fetch a short-lived token instead.' },
    ] },
  { k: 'multiple mounts', query: 'multiple mounts containerData pipe separated',
    q: ['can i have more than one volume', 'how do i mount a config file', 'how do i share a directory between components'],
    url: 'https://github.com/runonflux/flux/blob/master/docs/multiple-mounts-guide.md',
    doc: 'containerData uses pipe separated mount definitions. The primary mount is flags:container_path. A directory mount is m:subdirectory:container_path. A file mount is f:filename:container_path and is created as an empty file with 777 permissions, so the app must initialise its contents on first run. A component can reference another component with index:container_path, c:index:subdirectory:container_path or cf:index:filename:container_path, and may only reference components with a lower index in the compose array. You pay for total data used, not the number of mounts.',
    a: 'Yes. The `containerData` field takes several mounts separated by pipes [1]:\n\n```\nr:/usr/share/nginx/html|m:logs:/var/log/nginx|f:nginx.conf:/etc/nginx/nginx.conf\n```\n\n- `m:<subdir>:<path>` mounts an extra directory\n- `f:<file>:<path>` mounts a single file\n- `<index>:<path>`, `c:<index>:<subdir>:<path>` and `cf:<index>:<file>:<path>` reach into **another component** in the same app\n\nTwo rules that catch people. A component may only reference components **earlier in the compose array**, which keeps the creation order acyclic. And a file mount is created as an empty file, so your application has to detect a zero-byte config and write its defaults on first run.\n\nBilling is on total data used, not on the number of mounts, so extra mounts cannot be used to dodge the disk limit.' },
  { k: 'shared database', query: 'flux shared database mysql operator',
    q: ['what is the shared database', 'how do i run a database across instances', 'can i have a replicated mysql on flux'],
    url: 'https://docs.runonflux.com/fluxcloud/shared-database/',
    doc: 'Flux Shared DB bundles a standard database engine with a custom operator that discovers peers via FluxCloud, forms a master-slave cluster and exposes a native database interface on port 3307. The master sequences writes, slaves replicate, and reads are served locally by any node. It replicates across at least three FluxNodes. MySQL is fully supported; MongoDB and PostgreSQL are in progress. Deployment takes two components, the database engine container and the operator image runonflux/shared-db:latest. Instances must be between 3 and 100.',
    a: 'It is how you get a database that survives an instance moving [1]. You deploy two components: your database engine, and the operator image `runonflux/shared-db:latest`. The operator finds its peers through FluxCloud, forms a master-slave cluster and exposes the database on **port 3307**.\n\nWrites go to the master and are sequenced there. Reads are served locally by whichever node you hit, so reads scale with instances and writes stay globally ordered.\n\n**MySQL is fully supported.** MongoDB and PostgreSQL are listed as in progress, so do not plan around them yet.\n\nMinimum three instances, because that is what the replication is for. More slaves can slightly slow writes, since the master broadcasts to all of them.',
    fu: [
      { u: 'what resources does the operator need', doc: 'The operator component is recommended at 1 core, 1 GB RAM and 2 GB SSD, with three random public ports, container ports 3307, 7071 and 8008, and container data r:/app/dumps.',
        a: 'Small: 1 core, 1 GB of RAM, 2 GB of disk [1].\n\nThe fiddly part is its ports. It needs three public ports mapped to container ports **3307, 7071 and 8008**, with the domains field as three empty strings, and container data set to `r:/app/dumps`. The `r:` flag is what enables the Syncthing replication the dumps rely on.\n\nIt also needs `DB_COMPONENT_NAME` set to the internal hostname of your engine component, in the `flux<component>_<appname>` form.' },
    ] },
  { k: 'unlocking collateral', query: 'unlock fluxnode collateral zelcore',
    q: ['how do i unlock my fluxnode collateral', 'i want to stop running my node and get my flux back', 'how long does unlocking collateral take'],
    url: 'https://docs.runonflux.com/fluxnodes/unlocking-fluxnode-collateral',
    doc: 'Unlocking FluxNode collateral is done in Zelcore under Apps then FluxNodes: select the nodes and click Delete. The unlock usually completes within seconds and there is no documented waiting period. Do not refresh or leave the FluxNodes screen during the unlock, as the funds will re-lock and the procedure must be repeated. Unlocking does not affect the hardware. After unlocking, if the funds stay in the same wallet, send a transaction for an amount not equal to the collateral to break the exact output and prevent accidental relocking.',
    a: 'In Zelcore, under **Apps, then FluxNodes**: select the node and click Delete [1]. There is no waiting period, it usually completes in seconds.\n\nTwo things that go wrong.\n\n**Do not refresh or navigate away from the FluxNodes screen while it runs.** That re-locks the funds and you start over. This is the single most common complaint about the process.\n\nAnd afterwards, if the FLUX stays in the same wallet, send a transaction for an amount that is **not** the collateral figure, say 999.99 rather than 1,000. That breaks up the exact output so it cannot be picked up as collateral again by accident.\n\nThe machine is unaffected. You can power it off whenever you like.' },
  { k: 'parallel assets claim', query: 'claim parallel assets fusion zelcore chains',
    q: ['how do i claim my parallel assets', 'where are my parallel asset rewards', 'what chains are parallel assets on'],
    url: 'https://docs.runonflux.com/fluxnodes/claim-parallel-assets',
    doc: 'Parallel Assets are FluxNode rewards distributed on other blockchains through the Parallel Mining program, with 10 assets active across Kadena, Ethereum, BNB Smart Chain, Tron, Solana, Avalanche, Ergo, Algorand, Polygon and Base. Claiming is manual through the Fusion app inside Zelcore: Apps, Fusion, the three dot menu, Parallel Mining Claim, then select asset and mining address. Each claim incurs a network fee. If no mining address appears in the dropdown, the claimable balance is below the claim fee. Parallel mining launched 27 March 2021 and rewards for newly added assets are retroactively claimable.',
    a: 'Manually, through the **Fusion** app inside Zelcore [1]. Apps, then Fusion, then the three-dot menu, then Parallel Mining Claim. Pick the asset and the mining address, check the claimable amount against the fee, and claim.\n\nTen assets are active: Kadena, Ethereum, BNB Smart Chain, Tron, Solana, Avalanche, Ergo, Algorand, Polygon and Base.\n\nThe usual confusion: if no mining address appears in the dropdown, it is not broken. Your claimable balance on that chain is below the claim fee. Each claim costs a network fee on the destination chain, so let them accumulate and claim monthly or quarterly rather than chasing every reward.\n\nSSP can also do this, and consolidates the claim across chains into native FLUX.',
    fu: [
      { u: 'do i lose them if i dont claim for a year', a: 'No. They accumulate and rewards for newly added assets are even retroactively claimable back to the fork that added them [1]. Waiting is the recommended behaviour rather than a risk, because the fee is per claim.' },
    ] },
  { k: 'app management', query: 'flux cloud manage app control soft hard reinstall update cancel',
    q: ['whats the difference between soft and hard reinstall', 'how do i change my app after deploying it', 'can i transfer my app to someone else'],
    url: 'https://docs.runonflux.com/fluxcloud/applications/management/manage-app/control',
    doc: 'The Control tab offers Manual, Local and Global scopes with Start, Stop, Restart, Pause, Unpause, Remove Application, Soft Reinstall which preserves persistent data, and Hard Reinstall which wipes persistent data. Subscription Update cannot add new components but can change the owner field to transfer app ownership. A renewal toggle at Period is on by default and adds the selected duration on top of the current expiry. Cancel is irreversible: the app is immediately removed and all data permanently lost unless manually backed up, and remaining subscription time is forfeited.',
    a: '**Soft reinstall** pulls and recreates the container but keeps the persistent volume. **Hard reinstall** wipes the persistent data too [1]. Soft is what you want for a stuck container; hard is what you want when the data itself is the problem, and it is not recoverable.\n\nBoth are on the Control tab, which also has Start, Stop, Restart, Pause and Remove, and lets you apply an action to one node, the node you are viewing, or every instance.\n\nOn changing an app: you sign an updated specification. You **cannot add new components** in an update, but you can change almost everything else, including the `owner` field, which is how you transfer an app to someone.\n\nOne default to watch. The renewal toggle on the Period field is **on**, so an update also extends the term by the duration you pick, on top of what is left. Updating a 30-day app on day 10 with 30 days selected gives you 50 days, not 30. Turn it off and you keep the remaining 20.',
    fu: [
      { u: 'what happens if i cancel', a: 'It is immediate and irreversible [1]. The app is removed from the network, all data is permanently lost unless you backed it up yourself, and the remaining subscription time is forfeited. Billing stops.\n\nThere is no pause-and-resume for a subscription. If you want the app back later you register it again from scratch, which is why the Backup and Restore tab is worth using before you cancel anything.' },
      { u: 'is there a shell into my container', doc: 'The Secure Shell tab offers Linux by default, /bin/bash, /bin/ash, /bin/sh, Other and Custom, with optional user and environment toggles. Sessions are ephemeral. The Volume Browser lists the container filesystem from root with download, rename, edit, delete, upload and new folder actions, and changes apply instantly.',
        a: 'Yes, the **Secure Shell** tab [1]. You pick the shell, optionally a user as `user`, `user:group`, `uid` or `uid:gid`, and optionally environment variables. Sessions are ephemeral, so nothing you start there survives the tab closing.\n\nNext to it is the **Volume Browser**, which is often the more useful of the two: it lists the container filesystem from root and lets you download, rename, edit, delete and upload files, with a usage bar. Changes apply instantly. Uploads are single files only, so use a zip or tar for a folder.' },
    ] },
  { k: 'FluxDrive plans', query: 'fluxdrive plans pricing api',
    q: ['how much does fluxdrive cost', 'what are the fluxdrive plans', 'does fluxdrive have an api'],
    url: 'https://docs.runonflux.com/fluxcloud/fluxdrive/',
    doc: 'FluxDrive plans are STARTER 10 GB at $0.50 a month, STANDARD 50 GB at $2.50 a month, ELITE 100 GB at $5.00 a month, all at $0.0017 per GB per day, plus a custom PRO plan. Bandwidth is unlimited on all plans. The API base is https://api.fluxdrive.runonflux.io, all endpoints are POST with HTTP basic auth using the ZelID and an API key secret. Maximum file size is 5 GB. There are 10 API keys per subscription, keys are shown once, never expire and cannot be re-scoped.',
    a: 'Three fixed plans and a custom one [1]:\n\n| Plan | Space | Price |\n|---|---|---|\n| Starter | 10 GB | $0.50 a month |\n| Standard | 50 GB | $2.50 a month |\n| Elite | 100 GB | $5.00 a month |\n\nAll of them work out at $0.0017 per GB per day, and bandwidth is unlimited on every plan, which is the part that matters if you are serving files.\n\nThere is an API at `api.fluxdrive.runonflux.io`. Everything is POST with HTTP basic auth using your FluxID and an API key secret, and the scopes are read, write, list and delete. Maximum file size is 5 GB, you get 10 keys per subscription, and a key is shown once and can never be re-scoped, so write it down and scope it narrowly when you make it.' },
  { k: 'progressive node rewards', query: 'progressive node rewards arcaneos application revenue',
    q: ['do node operators get paid when apps run on them', 'what is pnr', 'why should i run arcaneos instead of the legacy node'],
    url: 'https://docs.runonflux.com/fluxnodes/what-are-fluxnodes',
    doc: 'Progressive Node Rewards are available to ArcaneOS nodes only: 80 percent of FluxCloud application revenue goes to ArcaneOS node operators and 20 percent to the ArcaneOS nodes actually hosting applications. Legacy nodes receive no Progressive Node Rewards.',
    a: 'Yes, through **Progressive Node Rewards**, and it is the strongest argument for ArcaneOS over the legacy install [1].\n\nApplication revenue is split 80/20: 80% is distributed across ArcaneOS node operators generally, and 20% goes to the ArcaneOS nodes actually hosting applications.\n\nLegacy nodes get none of it. They still earn block rewards, but the revenue from people deploying apps passes them by entirely. If you are running a legacy node today, that is the number to weigh against the effort of migrating.' },
  { k: 'managed nodes', query: 'managed fluxnode service third party hosting',
    q: ['can someone else run my node for me', 'what is the managed fluxnode service', 'is there node hosting'],
    url: 'https://docs.runonflux.com/fluxnodes/managed-fluxnode-service',
    doc: 'Third-party providers set up, host and maintain a FluxNode while the owner keeps custody of the FLUX collateral and can withdraw at any time. You supply collateral details including the transaction id, output index, ZelID and tier. Earnings go directly to your non-custodial wallet. Flux does not endorse any provider. Documented risks include monthly fees outweighing rewards on low tiers, trust and uptime risk, limited configurability, centralization risk and reported delays in refunds or service.',
    a: 'Yes, there are managed providers, and the model is reasonable: they run the machine, you keep the collateral [1]. You hand over the collateral transaction id, output index, your ZelID and the tier. Earnings go straight to your own wallet, and you can withdraw the collateral whenever you like.\n\nFlux does not endorse any particular provider and says so plainly. The risks the docs themselves list are worth reading before you sign up: on a Cumulus the monthly fee can eat most of the reward, you are trusting someone else\'s uptime, configurability is limited, it concentrates the network, and there have been reports of slow refunds.\n\nIf the appeal is not operating hardware rather than the hosting specifically, Titan does the same thing without a provider relationship.' },
  { k: 'flux ecosystem', query: 'flux ecosystem products influx technologies',
    q: ['what are all the flux products', 'what does influx technologies do', 'whats in the flux ecosystem'],
    url: 'https://docs.runonflux.com/home/flux-ecosystem',
    doc: 'The Flux ecosystem comprises FluxCloud for container deployment, FluxNodes as the infrastructure layer, FluxEdge as a GPU marketplace, FluxCore as the desktop provider application, Zelcore as a non-custodial multi-chain wallet, SSP Wallet as a browser extension with dual-signature transactions, FluxAI as a privacy-focused AI platform on FluxEdge GPUs comprising FluxGPT, FluxONE and FluxINTEL, and FusionX as a multi-chain DEX aggregator. InFlux Technologies, founded under the Zel banner in 2018 and rebranded in late 2022, is the company behind the ecosystem, led by CEO and co-founder Daniel Keller.',
    a: 'Eight things, and they divide cleanly into supply and demand [1].\n\n**Using capacity:** FluxCloud deploys containers on the CPU network. FluxEdge rents GPUs by the hour. FluxAI is a hosted AI product built on FluxEdge, covering FluxGPT for chat, FluxONE for images and FluxINTEL for documents.\n\n**Supplying capacity:** FluxNodes host FluxCloud applications and need locked collateral. FluxCore is the desktop app that offers your GPU and CPU to FluxEdge, and needs none.\n\n**Wallets and money:** Zelcore, the broad multi-chain wallet. SSP, the 2-of-2 multisignature one. FusionX, a multi-chain swap aggregator.\n\nAll of it comes from **InFlux Technologies**, which started under the Zel name in 2018 and took the current name in late 2022.',
    fu: [
      { u: 'is fluxai.io one of yours', a: 'No, and it is worth being clear about because the name invites the mistake. The Flux AI product is at **ai.runonflux.com**. A site at fluxai.io is unrelated to InFlux Technologies as far as the documentation goes, so do not treat anything there as official.\n\nIf you are ever unsure whether something is genuinely Flux, start from runonflux.com or the documentation and follow links out, rather than arriving from a search result or a message.' },
      { u: 'where is the official community', doc: 'The official social channels are Discord at discord.com/invite/runonflux, X at x.com/RunOnFlux, Telegram at t.me/runonflux and LinkedIn at linkedin.com/company/influxtechnologies.',
        a: 'Four official channels [1]: Discord at `discord.com/invite/runonflux`, X at `x.com/RunOnFlux`, Telegram at `t.me/runonflux`, and LinkedIn under `influxtechnologies`.\n\nThose are the ones listed in the docs. If you see a Flux support account anywhere else, particularly one that messages you first, treat it as fake.' },
    ] },
  { k: 'node service api', query: 'flux node service hostinfo endpoint',
    q: ['can my app find out what node its running on', 'how do i get the node ip from inside my container', 'is there an api for host info'],
    url: 'https://github.com/runonflux/flux/blob/master/docs/flux-node-service.md',
    doc: 'The Flux Node Service is an HTTP server on port 16101 giving Docker apps information about the node they run on. The single endpoint is GET http://fluxnode.service:16101/hostinfo with no headers required. Authentication is IP based: the service resolves the requesting container from the remote IP and it is not reachable from outside the Docker network. The response contains appName, id which is the collateral transaction hash concatenated with the output index, ip, geo and benchmark data.',
    a: 'Yes, and it is a nice piece of the platform that almost nobody knows about [1]:\n\n```\nGET http://fluxnode.service:16101/hostinfo\n```\n\nNo headers, no token. Authentication is by IP: the service works out which container is asking from the source address, and it is not reachable from outside the Docker network at all.\n\nYou get back your `appName`, the node\'s `id` which is the collateral transaction hash plus the output index, the public `ip`, a `geo` object with continent, country, region, city, coordinates and whether the node is a data centre or has a static IP, and a `benchmark` object with the node\'s cores, RAM, disk, disk write speed and measured up and down speeds.\n\nUseful for logging which node an instance landed on, or for picking behaviour based on the region you were scheduled into. The geolocation `org` and raw IP fields are stripped for privacy.' },
];
